<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01Q0">Definition <span data-tag="01Q0">4.8.4.8</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ and $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be simplicial categories and let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a simplicial functor. </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
We say that $F$ is <em>weakly fully faithful</em> if, for every pair of objects $X,Y \in \operatorname{Ob}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, the induced map $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(X,Y)_{\bullet } \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}( F(X), F(Y) )_{\bullet }$ is a weak homotopy equivalence of simplicial sets. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $F$ is <em>weakly essentially surjective</em> if the induced functor of homotopy categories $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{F}: \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}} \rightarrow \operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}}$ is essentially surjective (that is, every object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is homotopy equivalent to an object of the form $F(X)$, for some $X \in \operatorname{Ob}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$). 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $F$ is a <em>weak equivalence of simplicial categories</em> if it is weakly fully faithful and weakly essentially surjective. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
